+### Changed
+- **Options → Suspended tabs: grouped "don't restore scroll position" next to "keep YouTube playback position"** (`options.html`): both control what state gets restored on unsuspend, but the scroll-position checkbox sat several rows away, next to "reload also unsuspends in the background" and "discard after suspend" instead. Moved directly under the YouTube-timestamp checkbox so the two page-state-restoration options read together.
+- **Debug page: Controls section redesigned to cut wasted vertical space** (`debug.html`, `debug.css`, `debug.js`): the 7 option boxes were all forced to the height of the single tallest one via `equalizeCardHeights()` (mostly `captureLogs`'s 3-line description), even the ones with a single line of text. The two behavioural toggles with real explanations (`captureLogs`, `discardInPlaceOfSuspend`) keep the full 2-column card layout; the other 5 (claim suspended tabs, favicon cache, changelog modal reset, news feed, backup device identity) moved into a compact single-line row list. `equalizeCardHeights()` removed as dead code; the 2-card grid stretches evenly on its own via native CSS grid behaviour.
 
 ### Fixed
-- **`captureLogs` went silently blind after the Service Worker's first restart of a session** (`background.js`): the persisted `chrome.storage.local` flag was only restored inside `startupOnce()`, which runs once per browser session, not once per Service Worker spawn — since the in-memory flag resets to `false` on every SW recycle (routine in MV3), captureLogs stopped working after the first restart, undermining the debug reports it's meant to support. Moved the restore to unconditional top-level code so it runs on every wake. Also dropped a dead call to a since-removed `tgs.updateTabIdReferences` in the `onReplaced` listener.
-- **Favicon data URLs bloated the debug log buffer** (`gsUtils.js`): tab dumps logged via `captureLogs` (`checkQueue`'s "Updated tab" entries) serialized the whole tab object including `favIconUrl` as a base64 `data:` URL, often several KB of text per entry, evicting most of the 500-entry log buffer within seconds on tab-heavy sessions. Replaced with a length + short hash fingerprint so favicon changes between log lines are still comparable without the bloat.
-- **Popup's "Backup now" bypassed the 30s manual-backup cooldown** (`gsBackup.js`, `background.js`, `backup.js`, `popup.js`): the cooldown after a manual backup was only ever a per-page UI debounce local to `backup.html`'s own button — the popup's own "Backup now" menu item had no cooldown at all and didn't share state with the page, so triggering from both let a user fire off backups back-to-back with no pause. Moved the cooldown into `gsBackup.performManualBackup()`, tracked in `chrome.storage.session` so it's enforced regardless of which UI triggers it; `backup.html` now also checks for a cooldown already in progress (e.g. started from the popup moments earlier) when the page loads, and the popup disables its "Backup now" item and shows a live countdown if opened during one. The scheduled auto-backup alarm still calls `performBackup()` directly and is unaffected.
+- **Debug page: tabs whose content script stopped responding (page still alive, distinct from a discarded tab) stayed stuck reporting "unknown" indefinitely, surviving repeated page reloads** (`debug.js`): `getDebugInfo()` probed a "normal" tab's content script directly via `sendRequestInfoToContentScript()`, and on failure fed a `null` status into `calculateTabStatus()`, which *itself* attempted the exact same probe a second time — pure wasted duplicate work — before falling back to `STATUS_UNKNOWN` on a second failure, with no attempt to reinject the content script. Only `tgs.js`'s own periodic `checkQueue` responsiveness checks ever did that reinjection, entirely independent of this page, so a tab that broke between two of those scheduled runs had no way to recover via the debug page at all; confirmed via live testing, several tabs stuck reporting "unknown" for many minutes across repeated manual reloads. `getDebugInfo()` now routes the check through `gsTabCheckManager.queueTabCheckAsPromise()` — the same responsiveness-check queue `checkQueue` itself uses, reinjection attempt included — instead of a one-shot probe, and `calculateTabStatus()` no longer needs its own redundant attempt, since it's given the already-resolved status directly. Every page (including this one) gets its own separate `gsTabCheckManager` module instance, so `debug.js` now also calls `gsTabCheckManager.initAsPromised()` during its own init (the same pattern `health.js` already used for its own tab-suspend/check needs) — without it, `queueTabCheckAsPromise()` would silently hit the "queue not initialized" guard and resolve `STATUS_UNKNOWN` immediately every time, skipping the reinjection attempt entirely. A Codex review round then caught that the recovery still wasn't visible where it mattered: a tab needing actual reinjection routinely takes longer than the 500ms per-tab render deadline `fetchTabInfo()` already enforces, so that render still (correctly) shows "unknown" for it — but the late result, once it did arrive, was simply discarded, since only the raced `promiseWithTimeout()` value ever reached the table. The row stayed stuck showing the timeout fallback until some unrelated later refresh happened to run. Each tab's `<tr>` now carries a `data-tab-id` attribute, and the underlying (unraced) info promise patches that one row in place once it resolves, so a tab that recovers mid-check updates live instead of waiting for the next full profiler refresh. A further Codex review round caught two more, both real races the live-update fix above introduced. First: a late update from an *older* pass could overwrite a *newer* pass's already-correct row for the same tab (e.g. it genuinely became pinned in between) with stale data, since nothing tracked which pass a given row's data actually came from; every pass now stamps a generation counter, and a late update only applies if its own pass is still the newest one. Second, and more serious architecturally: this page's own separate `gsTabCheckManager` instance (`initAsPromised()`'d locally, added above) has no visibility into a recovery the *service worker's own* queue might already be running for the same tab (e.g. `tgs.js`'s `handleTabFocusChanged()`) — two independent queues could both decide to reinject the same tab's content script at once, and reinjection is already documented (`gsTabCheckManager.js`'s own comments) as leaving old runtime listeners active when that happens. The check is now routed through a new `checkTabResponsiveness` message, handled in `background.js` by calling the service worker's own singleton `gsTabCheckManager` queue instead — the same one everything else in the extension already funnels through — and `debug.js` no longer creates (or initialises) its own separate instance at all. A final Codex review round caught that the new `checkTabResponsiveness` message wasn't marked service-worker-only: `chrome.runtime.sendMessage` with no `tabId` broadcasts to every open extension page, so `options.html`/`updated.html`'s own listeners (which didn't know this new action, unlike `background.js`) could resolve first with an unhandled-message response, racing and beating the service worker's real, slower one — leaving `calculateTabStatus()` falling back to the old one-shot probe with the background-queued recovery no longer attached to the live row update. Added `checkTabResponsiveness` to the existing `gsUtils.INTERNAL_MESSAGE_ACTIONS` set every page's `messageRequestListener` already consults for exactly this class of problem.
+
+  On top of the review-driven fixes above, both `fetchTabInfo()` and `buildReport()` fired their whole per-tab batch via a plain `Promise.all(tabs.map(...))`, with no cap on how many ran at once — fine for a handful of tabs, but a profile with hundreds of them open would fire hundreds of concurrent `chrome.tabs.get()`/`chrome.alarms.get()` calls, and (for every "normal" tab) hundreds of simultaneous `checkTabResponsiveness` messages to the service worker, all in one instant, on every single profiler refresh or report generation. A new `mapWithConcurrency()` helper caps both to at most 20 tabs in flight at once, spreading that same total work out over time instead of bursting it — the debug page (and this whole log-buffer/tab-check machinery it now exercises) should stay usable rather than becoming the thing that finally tips a heavily-loaded profile (hundreds of tabs open) into crashing.
+
+  A Codex review round then caught two consequences of that same batching change. First, in `fetchTabInfo()`: with concurrency-limited batches, a tab's real result can now land in the gap between "no row exists yet" (the initial render hasn't happened) and "the final render already ran" — the live per-row patch is a silent no-op in that gap, and without also tracking the fresher value elsewhere, the eventual render still baked in that tab's stale timeout fallback despite already knowing better. `debugInfos` is now populated in place from the same unraced result the live patch already uses, so the final render always reflects whichever is freshest. Second, in `buildReport()`: the service worker's own `gsTabCheckManager` queue has only 3 concurrent executors, so with more than a handful of "normal" tabs, the existing 500ms per-tab deadline expired for most of them well before the queue even got to processing them — not because they were unresponsive, but because they were still waiting their turn — and unlike the live table, a generated report has no later-refresh path to correct a wrong "unknown" afterwards; it freezes whatever it captured permanently. Report generation dropped the per-tab timeout entirely (the queue's own 60s job timeout is still the real upper bound) — it's a deliberate one-off action, unlike the live table, so it's fine for it to take longer in exchange for actually being correct.
+- **Debug page: repeated `focus` events could stack overlapping full tab-info refreshes, flooding the log and hammering every open tab** (`debug.js`): `window.addEventListener('focus', () => { fetchTabInfo(); refreshLogs(); })` had no debounce or re-entrancy guard, but Chrome/the OS can genuinely fire several `focus` events on this page in quick succession (multi-monitor setups, rapid alt-tabbing, etc.). Each one kicked off its own full `fetchTabInfo()` run, and every one of those calls `getDebugInfo()` — including a real `sendRequestInfoToContentScript()` round trip for every "normal" (non-suspended) tab — for every currently open tab again, all overlapping. Confirmed via a live debug report spanning several days of `captureLogs` left on: a burst of duplicate `getDebugInfo`/`getDebugInfo callback` log lines for the same tab within the same millisecond, repeated roughly once per stray focus event, on top of everything already fixed in the log-buffer rework — a genuinely separate cause of both the sluggishness and repeated crashes. `fetchTabInfo()` now skips re-entrant calls while one is already in flight, since a later call arriving mid-refresh is redundant anyway. A Codex review round then caught that dropping a re-entrant call outright is also wrong: if the user left and came back (opened/closed/changed a tab) while a run was still in flight, that run's `tabsQuery()` snapshot was already taken before the change, so it renders stale by the time it finishes, and the profiler stayed stale until another focus event happened to occur. A re-entrant call now queues one follow-up run instead of dropping outright, with further calls arriving while that follow-up is already queued still coalescing into it — a rapid burst still produces at most one extra run, not one per stray event.
+- **Debug page: `options.html`/`updated.html` could still shadow the service worker's `checkTabResponsiveness` response even after that action was added to `INTERNAL_MESSAGE_ACTIONS`, and a tab closing mid-check could stall the whole report** (`options.js`, `updated.js`, `debug.js`, #472 review): both pages' `messageRequestListener` were declared `async`, so their early `return false;` decline of this service-worker-only action was still wrapped in a resolved Promise — Chrome/Firefox can treat a listener's returned Promise as its actual response, letting that trivial resolved value race and beat the service worker's real, slower result. Both listeners are now synchronous functions, matching the pattern `background.js`/`suspended.js` already use. Separately, `getDebugInfo()` left its callback uninvoked when a tab closed between `tabsQuery()` and its own `chrome.tabs.get()` lookup — the async function's rejection had nothing catching it, leaving `fetchTabInfo()`'s/`buildReport()`'s manually constructed Promise pending forever now that the per-tab timeout was removed from the report path; it now resolves an unknown-status fallback instead. That fallback also surfaced a latent crash in `generateTabInfo()`, which read `info.tab.url` without checking `info.tab` first when no cached favicon data URL was available. A further Codex review round then caught that making `options.js`'s listener synchronous exposed a separate, real bug: `tgs.js`'s `handleNewStationaryTabFocus()` awaits an `initSettings` message to `options.html` before resetting the previously focused tab's suspend timer, but that case never called `sendResponse()` — with the listener now returning literal `true` (rather than an async function's implicitly-resolving Promise), the message channel stayed open with nothing ever responding, hanging that await until the channel itself tore down and leaving the previous tab's timer un-reset in the meantime. `sendResponse()` is now called unconditionally after the switch, since every case there is already fully synchronous. A further Codex review round then caught that calling it unconditionally overcorrected: on the Chrome versions this manifest still supports (110-121), an async listener's returned Promise wasn't treated as a response at all, but this synchronous listener's unconditional `sendResponse()` now actively answers *every* action outside `INTERNAL_MESSAGE_ACTIONS` with `undefined` — including ones this page doesn't own, like `debug.js`'s `repairFavicons` (handled only in `background.js`), where this stray response could beat the real, slower one and make the debug UI report `failed` despite the repair actually succeeding. `sendResponse()` now only runs in the `initSettings` case; the `default` case goes back to a real `false` decline instead. A further Codex review round then caught a separate bug in the concurrency-limited batching itself, unrelated to the message-listener issues above: each `mapWithConcurrency()` worker released its concurrency slot as soon as the 500ms render deadline passed, even though the underlying `infoPromise` (and its `checkTabResponsiveness` message to the service worker) kept running in the background. With hundreds of "normal" tabs slower than 500ms, `mapWithConcurrency()` raced ahead into the next batch on every one of those releases while the slow tabs' real checks stayed in flight — batch after batch piling up hundreds of in-flight messages and open response channels against the service worker's own queue (only 3 concurrent executors), exactly the burst this concurrency cap exists to prevent. Each worker now still `await`s the real `infoPromise` before releasing its slot, keeping the 500ms deadline scoped to only what gets rendered in that pass. A further Codex review round then caught that this fix traded one problem for another: holding the slot until the real check settled meant `mapWithConcurrency()`'s own returned promise — which `fetchTabInfo()` awaits before rendering anything,