+- **Log buffer: a burst of near-simultaneous flushes from many open contexts (e.g. every suspended tab reacting to `captureLogs` being toggled) could make the whole extension sluggish, including unrelated page loads** (`gsUtils.js`): every `gsAppendLogEntries` message costs one full get+parse+stringify+set of the entire shared buffer (up to `_LOG_BUFFER_FULL_MAX` entries) — `chrome.storage.local` has no append primitive, only whole-value writes. A broadcast every open context reacts to at once (toggling `captureLogs` itself, which every suspended tab logs as "ignoring unhandled message") could land dozens of these messages within a few milliseconds of each other; merging each one separately serialized dozens of full-buffer round trips back to back through `_writeQueue`, each one's synchronous JSON work blocking this single-threaded context in turn — including its ability to promptly respond to anything else, like whatever `options.html`/`debug.html` needed from it on their own load. Confirmed via live testing with ~50 suspended tabs open and `captureLogs` on: reopening the debug/options pages became noticeably slow, worse than before this rework began. Incoming messages are now coalesced into one merge per 250ms window instead of one per message, turning a 50-tab burst into a single round trip; the live/report buffer size caps (`_LOG_BUFFER_MAX`/`_LOG_BUFFER_FULL_MAX`) are left unchanged, since a user with many tabs open is exactly the case where a shorter history would hurt diagnosing an issue the most — the coalescing alone removes the multiplier, independent of buffer size. A GitHub Codex review round then caught a severe regression the coalescing itself introduced: if many contexts had each independently accumulated up to their own `_pendingEntries` cap (5,000) while the service worker was unreachable, all flushing once it came back reachable could aggregate well over 100,000 entries into a single coalesced batch — and `_mergeAndPersistCore()` appended it via `current.push(...freshEntries)`, a variadic spread that turns every element into its own function argument and throws a `RangeError` in V8 past a few tens of thousands of them. The merge then failed, every sender requeued its batch, and the next coalesced window reproduced the exact same oversized aggregate, indefinitely — a real, reproducible crash matching a live report of the extension crashing after `captureLogs` had been left on for a while, buffer around 5,000 entries. Fixed two ways: `_mergeAndPersistCore()` now appends via `concat()`, which has no argument-count limit regardless of array size, and `_flushIncomingBatch()` now trims the aggregated batch itself down to `_LOG_BUFFER_FULL_MAX` (keeping the newest) before it's ever merged, since carrying more of it into the merge than the persisted buffer could retain anyway was pure waste. A local adversarial-review round then flagged that trimming only at flush time still let the aggregate grow unbounded, even if briefly, throughout the whole 250ms window while messages keep arriving — the exact CPU/memory spike this coalescing exists to avoid, just delayed a beat. `_incomingBatch` is now capped on every single arrival (not just once before merging), and appended via `concat()` rather than a spread there too, since a single sender's own entries array can already be large enough (up to its own 5,000-entry cap) to risk the same argument-count limit on its own. Another GitHub Codex review round then caught an ordering race the coalescing design introduced: the service worker's own logging (`error()` in particular, which flushes immediately and bypasses the incoming-message coalescing entirely) merges through `_mergeAndPersistCore()` via a separate call path from a page's coalesced batch, and both end up serialized by `_writeQueue` in whichever order they happened to be *called*, not the order their entries were actually logged in — a page's batch that had been waiting out its 250ms window could land after a genuinel