+- **`.gitignore` didn't actually ignore the local OAuth client-secret file** (`.gitignore`): the gitignored-by-convention, never-committed file holding the PKCE Drive-auth client secret (#437) had no matching rule, so it showed up as a plain untracked file ready to be swept into a careless `git add -A`. Added, along with `/memory` (a local notes directory, same reasoning). Superseded later in this same release by the `gsOauthSecrets.js`/`gsOauthSecrets.local.js` split described above, but the underlying `.gitignore` gap this fixed (an ignore rule that looked right but silently didn't match) was real and worth keeping as its own note.
+- **Log buffer: eager full-buffer load in every page, concurrent writers clobbering each other's entries, captureLogs never applying outside the service worker, stale captureLogs in already-open pages, a self-sustaining broadcast loop, an unhandled split-incognito writer, a collision-prone version counter, a Clear-log race, silent data loss on persistent failures, an async guard racing its own dedicated listener, an incomplete Clear-vs-merge race, a clear failure that could permanently wedge the write queue, a still-incomplete Clear-vs-merge purge, a corrupt buffer that could wedge retries forever, Clear's own unconditional remove able to delete a legitimately newer merge, a non-array parsed buffer able to wedge retries the same way, a failed cutoff write that could still report Clear as successful, a malformed array element able to wedge retries the same way again, a failed clear never actually reaching the debug page, and an unhandled rejection able to leave a message channel hanging** (`gsUtils.js`, `background.js`, `debug.js`, `options.js`, `suspended.js`, `updated.js`, #463/#464 review): twenty-two issues in the log-buffer rework, found across thirteen rounds of Codex review. `gsUtils.js` is imported by every page including `suspended.js`, so every context (service worker, every suspended tab, options, debug, etc.) gets its own separate module instance with its own in-memory buffer. `_ensureBufferLoaded()` used to hydrate that buffer from persisted storage (up to 10,000 entries) eagerly at module load, so every open suspended tab loaded the whole thing into memory just by being open, working against the extension's whole point; removed, since `_flushNow()` only actually needs it lazily. `clearLogBuffer()` cleared the in-memory arrays and fired off `chrome.storage.local.remove()` without awaiting it, racing an in-flight hydration that could repopulate the arrays with the old entries right after. The persisted `captureLogs` flag was restored only in `background.js`, so `gsChangelog.js`'s diagnostic `gsUtils.warning()` calls (added specifically to make changelog-modal failures diagnosable) never buffered anything running in `options.js`'s context, since that context's own `captureLogs` copy never got restored; and toggling captureLogs on the debug page only messaged the service worker, so an already-open page kept whatever value it loaded with. Fixing those two surfaced the deepest issue: with multiple contexts actually flushing now, each one overwrote storage with its own full local snapshot (or, in an intermediate append-merge attempt, still raced another context's uncoordinated read-modify-write), so two contexts logging around the same time could silently erase what the other had just written, and chrome.storage has no compare-and-swap to fix that with more retries. Settled on a single-writer design instead: only the service worker (`_isServiceWorker`, detected via `self instanceof ServiceWorkerGlobalScope`) ever touches these two storage keys; every other context hands its pending entries to it via a `gsAppendLogEntries` runtime message instead of writing directly. Inside the service worker, every write (a merge or a clear) is chained through one `_writeQueue` promise, so its own scheduled flush and an incoming message can no longer interleave their storage round trips either. `clearLogBuffer()` (only ever called from the service worker) now goes through that same queue, so it's correctly ordered against anything already merged or in flight instead of racing it. The `captureLogs` restore moved into `gsUtils.js` itself so every context picks up the persisted value on load, and a `chrome.storage.onChanged` listener there keeps it in sync live afterward, no per-page wiring needed for either. A fourth round then caught a self-sustaining loop the single-writer message actually introduced: `chrome.runtime.sendMessage` with no `tabId` broadcasts to every page, so every open suspended tab's (and `options.js`'s and `updated.js`