s own `_pendingEntries` (or still mid-flight) at the exact moment "Clear log" runs could still land, and get persisted, right after the clear completed, resurrecting the entries the user just wiped — cancelling a message already in transit isn't something the extension messaging API offers a hook for. `_clearPersisted()` now also writes a `gsLogBufferClearedAt` timestamp (never removed by the clear itself), and every merge attempt drops entries older than it before persisting, regardless of which side of the clear the merge physically lands on. The same round also caught that a batch failing to persist after all 5 version-conflict retries, or a service worker unreachable mid-flight, silently discarded that diagnostic history for good; `_flushNow()` now requeues a failed batch at the front of `_pendingEntries` and lets the next scheduled flush retry it instead of losing it. A sixth round caught three more: `background.js`'s `messageRequestListener` was still declared `async` at the top (a separate instance of the same "async listener returns a Promise instead of the literal `true`/`false` Chrome needs" issue as elsewhere in this rework), so its early, synchronous-looking `return false;` for `gsAppendLogEntries` was actually wrapped in a Promise that could resolve before `gsUtils.js`'s own dedicated (and slower, since it does real storage I/O) listener responded; the sender keeps only whichever response arrives first, so `_flushNow()` could see that stray `false`, requeue an already-successfully-persisted batch, and resend (and re-persist) it every 1.5s indefinitely. `messageRequestListener` is now a synchronous function that returns `true`/`false` for real, running its async body in an internal IIFE. Second, the new `gsLogBufferClearedAt` cutoff only filtered the entries being merged, not the buffer state they were merged onto: if a merge's `get()` landed between Clear's two writes (the timestamp, then the buffer removal), it would still read the pre-clear buffer, and if its own `set()` then landed after Clear's removal, it silently reintroduced that entire pre-clear buffer while still verifying its own write as successful. `_mergeAndPersist()` now also re-checks `gsLogBufferClearedAt` as part of verification, retrying if it moved since the read, not just checking the write token. Third, `_clearPersisted()` had no error handling around its two storage calls; a rejected `chrome.storage` call there would leave `_writeQueue` itself a rejected promise, and since every future `_mergeAndPersist()`/`_clearPersisted()` call chains onto it with `.then()` and no rejection handler, that one failure would silently wedge all logging (merges and clears alike) until the service worker next restarted. Wrapped in `try`/`catch` to keep the queue alive across a failed clear, matching the resilience `_mergeAndPersist()` already had. A seventh round caught two more, both in the same function: the previous round's `clearedAt` re-check only re-filtered the incoming `entries`, not the persisted `current`/`currentFull` themselves — a worker whose earlier attempt had already written a stale pre-clear snapshot to storage (see the sixth round above) would, on noticing the clear and retrying, filter its own `entries` down to nothing and return early without ever purging that already-written stale snapshot, so Clear could still end up undone. Every attempt now re-filters the persisted buffers against `clearedAt` too, and writes the cleaned result even when the incoming batch itself has nothing new to add. Separately, a corrupt persisted buffer (malformed JSON) threw on every one of the 5 retry attempts without ever being recovered, so `_flushNow()` requeued the same unpersistable batch indefinitely, retrying roughly every 1.5s forever and blocking every later log entry from ever being persisted either. Each buffer is now parsed defensively and replaced with an empty array if corrupt, the same recovery the old (now-removed) buffer loader already had. An eigh