sed limitation rather than fixed here: replacing a wall-clock cutoff with a clock-independent generation/sequence marker is a larger, more invasive change than anything else in this pass, the failure mode (a machine's clock being corrected backward, immediately followed by "Clear log") is narrow, and the impact is limited to the debug-only log buffer, not any tab-suspend behaviour. A tenth round then caught that the previous round's unification (Clear now calls the same merge machinery as a normal write) had a gap of its own: if the `gsLogBufferClearedAt` write itself failed, the code still unconditionally went on to the purge step, which filtered against whatever cutoff was already in storage (unchanged, since the new one never landed), found nothing to purge, and reported success — so a failed Clear looked identical to a successful one from `clearLogBuffer()`'s side, and the debug page would refresh showing every old entry still there despite believing it had just been cleared. The purge now only runs when the cutoff write actually succeeded; on failure it reports `false` directly. Implementing that safely needed a small refactor: `_mergeAndPersist()`'s retry loop was pulled out into a separate `_mergeAndPersistCore()` that doesn't chain onto `_writeQueue` itself, since calling the queued `_mergeAndPersist()` wrapper reactively from inside `_clearPersisted()`'s own already-queued callback would have read `_writeQueue`'s value at whatever point that callback happened to run (by then possibly already advanced by other operations queued after it), risking a circular wait between the two steps; `_clearPersisted()` now calls the unchained core directly within its own single queued step instead. An eleventh round then caught a third variant of the same "corrupt buffer wedges retries forever" failure mode from two rounds ago, one layer deeper: even a syntactically valid array can contain a malformed *element* (`null`, a stray primitive, an object with a non-string `ts`, however it got there), which reached the `e.ts > clearedAt` comparison during filtering and threw there instead, with the same indefinite-retry consequence as an outright parse failure or a non-array top level. `parseBuffer()` now filters out anything that isn't a well-formed entry (an object with a string `ts`) after parsing, closing this specific class of corruption at the element level instead of only the top level. A twelfth round then caught that `_clearPersisted()`'s now-genuine `true`/`false` result (from the tenth round above) still never left `gsUtils.js`: `clearLogBuffer()` discarded it, `background.js`'s `clearLogs` case never set a response, and the debug page's "Clear log" button always just refreshed the log view regardless, so a failed clear (a transient storage error, or exhausted purge retries) looked identical to a successful one, with no indication the old entries were still there. `clearLogBuffer()` now returns the result instead of discarding it, `background.js` responds with `{ success }`, and "Clear log" briefly shows "clear failed" on the button (mirroring "repair favicons now"'s existing result-text pattern) instead of silently refreshing as if nothing were wrong. A thirteenth round then caught a gap in the sixth round's own async-IIFE fix: if any awaited call inside the switch threw (`performTabChecks()` failing, an unexpected manual-backup error, etc.), the detached IIFE simply rejected with nothing catching it, so `sendResponse()` never ran — since the outer listener had already returned `true` to keep the channel open, the sender was left waiting until the message port itself eventually tore down instead of promptly seeing the failure. The switch is now wrapped in `try`/`catch`, logging the error and still calling `sendResponse()` so the channel is always settled either way.
