 ### Fixed
-- **Suspended tabs kept the default TMS favicon after a browser restart until clicked** (`background.js`, #397): the startup checks that repair favicons/titles on every suspended tab (`gsSession.runStartupChecks`) only ran from the service worker `activate` event (install/update only) and `chrome.runtime.onStartup`, which some Chromium builds (notably Brave) never fire after a normal restart — leaving suspended tabs stuck with the generic icon until manually focused. Added a fallback that checks a `chrome.storage.session` sentinel (cleared at the browser-session boundary) on every service worker wake and runs the startup checks once if it's missing, regardless of whether `onStartup` fired. Thanks @pbc-commits for the investigation and root-cause writeup.
+- **`captureLogs` went silently blind after the Service Worker's first restart of a session** (`background.js`): the persisted `chrome.storage.local` flag was only restored inside `startupOnce()`, which runs once per browser session, not once per Service Worker spawn, since the in-memory flag resets to `false` on every SW recycle (routine in MV3), captureLogs stopped working after the first restart, undermining the debug reports it's meant to support. Moved the restore to unconditional top-level code so it runs on every wake. Also dropped a dead call to a since-removed `tgs.updateTabIdReferences` in the `onReplaced` listener.
+- **Favicon data URLs bloated the debug log buffer** (`gsUtils.js`): tab dumps logged via `captureLogs` (`checkQueue`'s "Updated tab" entries) serialized the whole tab object including `favIconUrl` as a base64 `data:` URL, often several KB of text per entry, evicting most of the 500-entry log buffer within seconds on tab-heavy sessions. Replaced with a length + short hash fingerprint so favicon changes between log lines are still comparable without the bloat.
+- **Popup's "Backup now" bypassed the 30s manual-backup cooldown** (`gsBackup.js`, `background.js`, `backup.js`, `popup.js`): the cooldown after a manual backup was only ever a per-page UI debounce local to `backup.html`'s own button; the popup's own "Backup now" menu item had no cooldown at all and didn't share state with the page, so triggering from both let a user fire off backups back-to-back with no pause. Moved the cooldown into `gsBackup.performManualBackup()`, tracked in `chrome.storage.session` so it's enforced regardless of which UI triggers it; `backup.html` now also checks for a cooldown already in progress (e.g. started from the popup moments earlier) when the page loads, and the popup disables its "Backup now" item and shows a live countdown if opened during one. The scheduled auto-backup alarm still calls `performBackup()` directly and is unaffected.
+- **Suspended tabs kept the default TMS favicon after a browser restart until clicked** (`background.js`, #397): the startup checks that repair favicons/titles on every suspended tab (`gsSession.runStartupChecks`) only ran from the service worker `activate` event (install/update only) and `chrome.runtime.onStartup`, which some Chromium builds (notably Brave) never fire after a normal restart, leaving suspended tabs stuck with the generic icon until manually focused. Added a fallback that checks a `chrome.storage.session` sentinel (cleared at the browser-session boundary) on every service worker wake and runs the startup checks once if it's missing, regardless of whether `onStartup` fired. Thanks @pbc-commits for the investigation and root-cause writeup.
 - **"Claim by default" option unclear even to technical users** (`options.html`, #151): the checkbox crammed its explanation into a long parenthetical in the label itself. Shortened the label and moved the explanation into a proper tooltip (same pattern already used elsewhere on the page): it takes over tabs already suspended by another compatible extension (an old TGS install, or a different TMS install) by rewriting their `suspended.html` URL to this extension. Translated across all 16 non-English/Italian locales.
 - **"Allow access to file URLs" toggle missing on every Chromium browser** (`manifest.json`, #393, #416, #267): Chrome only shows this toggle on an extension's details page when the manifest declares `file://` host access; TMS's `host_permissions` only ever listed `http://*/*`/`https://*/*`, so the toggle was invisible everywhere (confirmed on both fresh Chrome and Brave installs, not a Brave-specific quirk as first assumed). Added `"file:///*"` to a new `optional_host_permissions`, same on-demand pattern as `downloads`/`identity`, no access is granted until the user explicitly flips the toggle.
-- **Google Drive "Connect account" fails or hangs on Brave and Vivaldi** (`gsBackup.js`, #420): `chrome.identity.getAuthToken()` doesn't work on these Chromium forks, for two different reasons confirmed by manual testing. **Brave** rejects it outright: it attaches a custom URI scheme to the OAuth request that Google's backend no longer accepts (brave/brave-browser#38066, open since May 2024, unassigned, no fix timeline), and Brave's own implementation additionally opens a visible native tab that hits Google's servers and shows the raw `Error 400: invalid_request` page before failing, not something extension code can suppress. **Vivaldi** disables Google's browser sign-in entirely for privacy reasons, and its `getAuthToken()` callback simply never fires in that state (`"The user turned off browser signin"` only ever appears as an unread `chrome.runtime.lastError` in the console), silently hanging forever with no UI at all. Fixed by adding `chrome.identity.launchWebAuthFlow()` as a fallback, used as follows without changing anything for installs where `getAuthToken()` already works: `navigator.brave.isBrave()` is checked once up front to skip straight to the fallback on Brave (avoiding that doomed first attempt and its ugly tab), `getAuthTokenViaChromeIdentity()` is now raced against a timeout (6s for silent/background calls, which should never show any UI at all; 45s for the interactive "Connect" click, generous enough for a real consent flow) so a hung callback like Vivaldi's still surfaces as a failure instead of hanging forever, and any other failure from an interactive click falls back to `launchWebAuthFlow()` too, covering any other Chromium fork with a similar issue we haven't hit yet. Once the fallback succeeds for an install, that's remembered (`chrome.storage.local`) so later calls (including background auto-backup) go straight to it instead of re-probing a method already known to fail. Fallback tokens are cached in `chrome.storage.session` with their expiry and silently refreshed when possible. Requires a second OAuth client in Google Cloud Console, type "Web application", with `https://<extension-id>.chromiumapp.org/` registered as an authorized redirect URI (the existing "Chrome App" client keeps serving `getAuthToken()` unchanged). The Backup page now also prints which method is active as a second line under the "TMS Backups" folder name (e.g. "Chrome Identity API (getAuthToken)"), not translated and not a separate row — same treatment as the folder name itself, just a quick way for support to tell which OAuth path an install is on.
