 ## [9.0.0] — 2026-06-24 (session backup)
 
-### Localization
-- **All 16 non-English/Italian locales completed** (`src/_locales/*/messages.json`): after PR #400 synced every locale's key set to match `en`, a large batch of strings introduced by recent features (Tab Health, Session Backup, News feed — ~164 keys) turned out to be an unedited copy of the English source in *every single locale*, not just a few. On top of that, `cs`, `sk` and `ja` were mostly untranslated overall (195/373, 127/373 and 167/373 keys respectively), and `fr-FR` was missing 141 keys outright plus had ~200 more left as English copies. Filled in translations for all of it directly (`fr-FR` reuses the now-complete `fr` translations, since it was a neglected duplicate rather than a genuinely distinct regional variant). A small number of remaining "identical to English" strings are legitimate cognates/loanwords (e.g. "min", "Local", "Sessions", "Backup" in several languages), not gaps. Will be reconciled back into Crowdin via its "sync from GitHub" import.
-
-### Fixed
-- **Backup page: deleted Drive backups stayed selectable in the restore dropdown** (`backup.js`): deleting a file from "File di backup su Drive" (`buildFileRow`'s delete button) removed the row from that list but never touched the separate `driveBackupsSelect` restore dropdown, so a just-deleted backup remained pickable there until the page was manually refreshed — risking a restore attempt against a file that no longer exists. The delete handler now also removes the matching `<option>` from the restore dropdown (and its parent `<optgroup>` if left empty, and hides the restore card entirely if no backups remain).
-- **Backup page: Drive backup dropdown showed a mangled time (`10 30:00` instead of `10:30:00`)** (`backup.js`): `formatOptionLabel` converted the filename's `HH-MM-SS` segment to a display time with a regex that only fixed up the last `MM-SS` pair, leaving a space where the `HH:MM` colon should be. Now splits the date and time segments on `T` and colon-joins the whole time part, correctly handling both current (`HH-MM-SS`) and legacy (`HH-MM`) filename formats.
-- **Session backup: pinned state and real titles lost on restore** (`gsBackup.js`): `buildExportObject` only stored `url` and `groupId` per tab, so every restore from a local or Drive backup fell back to the tab's URL as its title and force-unpinned every tab. Now stores `title` (via `gsUtils.getCleanTabTitle`, same helper used for session history) and `pinned` per tab, and `importBackupJson` reads them back instead of hardcoding `title: tab.url` / `pinned: false`.
-- **News feed: scheduled background refresh never populated the cache** (`gsNewsFeed.js`): `parseRssXml` used `DOMParser`, which doesn't exist in the extension's MV3 service worker (`background.js`) — the alarm-driven and startup `fetchAndCache()` calls threw and were silently swallowed by the existing `catch`, so the feed (and unread badge) only ever updated when a user had `news.html` open. Replaced DOM-based parsing with regex-based RSS/HTML extraction that has no DOM dependency, so background refreshes now work.
-- **Session backup ran in split-incognito context** (`gsBackup.js`): `performBackup()` had no incognito guard, so the separate service-worker instance that Chrome spins up for the extension in split-incognito mode could schedule and run its own backup alarm — exporting incognito-only tab URLs to the local downloads folder or Google Drive using the same (shared, `chrome.storage.sync`-based) backup settings. `performBackup()` now returns early when `chrome.extension.inIncognitoContext` is true.
-- **Backup alarm not resynced after settings restore** (`backup.js`): importing settings (local file or Drive restore) wrote `AUTO_BACKUP_ENABLED`/interval/time directly to storage but never rescheduled the Chrome alarm, so an imported change to auto-backup only took effect after the next full browser/extension restart. `applySettingsJson()` now calls `gsBackup.syncAlarmWithSettings()` after saving.
-- **Drive UI briefly showed stale destination after switching Local → Drive** (`backup.js`): `updateDriveAuthUI()` was called before `saveChange()` persisted the newly selected destination, so it read back the old value from storage and kept the Drive restore/settings panels hidden until a reload. Reordered so `updateDriveAuthUI()` runs after the new value is saved.
-- **About page: doubled punctuation in the fork credit sentence** (`en/messages.json`): `html_about_fork_end` started with a leading comma (`", more information available on"`), which combined with the literal `"). "` already in `about.html` produced `"...(issue #1263). , more information..."`. Removed the leading comma and capitalized the sentence to `"More information available on"`, matching the Italian locale which was already correct.
-- **Backup page: session backup settings laid out as a two-column label/control grid** (`backup.html`, `backup.css`): device name, interval, destination and max files to keep now sit in a `backupSettingsGrid` (`grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr`) instead of a wrapping flex row, so every field's label lines up in a left column and its control in a right column — consistent alignment regardless of label length, no more unpredictable wrapping.
-- **Backup page: device name field capped at 30 characters** (`backup.html`, `backup.css`): reduced `maxlength` on the device name input from 40 to 30.
-- **Backup page: "Connected account" labels now end with a colon like the settings grid** (`backup.html`): "Account"/"Cartella" (`html_backup_drive_label_account`, `html_backup_drive_label_folder`) were missing the trailing `:` that every other `.topLabel` in the page has, making them look visually different even after sharing the same CSS class.
-- **Backup page: device name field shows the device ID as placeholder** (`backup.js`, `en/messages.json`, `it/messages.json`): when the device name field is empty, its placeholder now shows the actual device ID (e.g. `a91ca462`, same value shown on the debug page) instead of a generic "e.g. MacBook, Work PC" hint — since that ID is exactly what gets used as the fallback label wherever no custom name is set. Removed the now-unused `html_backup_device_name_placeholder` locale key.
-- **Backup page: "Backup now" and Drive connect/disconnect actions on the same row** (`backup.html`, `backup.css`): `driveAuthActions` moved inside `backupConfigAction` instead of sitting in its own row below it, so the two related actions (run a backup now, manage the Drive connection) sit side by side.
-- **Backup page: "Connected account" merged into the settings grid instead of its own box** (`backup.html`, `backup.js`, `backup.css`, `en/messages.json`, `it/messages.json`): the separate "Account collegato"/"Connected account" section (its own heading, `<dl>` grid, connect/disconnect buttons) is gone. Account and folder now appear as two more rows in `backupSettingsGrid`, shown only when the destination is Drive and a Drive account is connected, via nested `display: contents` wrapper elements (`.driveGridRows`) that let a parent/child pair of hidden toggles flatten into the same grid without breaking its row/column layout. The connect/disconnect buttons moved to a new `driveAuthActions` row below "Backup now", following the same settings-vs-action separation as the rest of the page. Removed the now-unused `html_options_backup_drive_account` locale key.
-- **Backup page: "Connected account" (Drive) grid now matches the settings grid style** (`backup.html`, `backup.css`): `.driveInfoGrid` (`dt`/`dd` list for account and folder) switched from a custom mono/uppercase label style and a `1fr` stretching second column to reusing the shared `.topLabel` class and the same `max-content minmax(0, 280px)` layout as `.backupSettingsGrid`, so both grids on the page look and behave the same way.
-- **Backup page: all settings controls now align to the same right edge** (`backup.css`): the grid's control column switched from `1fr` (stretching to fill the panel) to a fixed `minmax(0, 280px)`, and the device name input went back to `flex: 1` within that column instead of a fixed `22ch`. Interval, destination and max files selects already fill 100% of the column, so the device name input + "Save" button row now spans exactly the same width as the other fields instead of overshooting them.
-- **Backup page: device name field now always visible, marked optional, and shown first** (`backup.html`, `backup.js`, `en/messages.json`, `it/messages.json`): the "Device name" field on the backup page was previously hidden unless other devices were already present in the Drive device registry, so it could appear unexpectedly after a fresh install once old multi-device backups were detected. It's now always shown, positioned above interval/destination/max files, with the label updated to "Device name (optional)" / "Nome dispositivo (opzionale)" to clarify it's not required.
-- **Backup page: device name Save button height mismatch** (`backup.css`): the `btnSmall` class used on the device name row's "Save" button had no actual styling, so it fell back to the default 40px-tall `.btn`, which didn't match the shorter text input next to it. Both elements are now aligned to the same height.
-- **Backup page: Google Drive restore row wrapping onto two lines** (`backup.css`): `.driveBackupPickerRow` used `flex-wrap: wrap` while the dropdown had a fixed `min-width: 150px`, causing the select and "Import selected" button to wrap in narrower cards. The dropdown now shrinks to fit instead of wrapping.
-- **Health page: localization now respects TMS language setting** (`health.js`, `gsUtils.js`): all dynamic scan-result strings in the Tab Health page were using `chrome.i18n.getMessage()` (browser locale) instead of `gsUtils.getMessage()` (user-selected TMS locale). Replaced all calls; extended `gsUtils.getMessage()` to handle placeholder substitutions so the repair/restore button labels stay correct.
-- **Shortcuts page: command descriptions ignored the TMS language setting** (`shortcuts.js`): `chrome.commands.getAll()` returns each command's `description` already resolved by Chrome using the *browser's* UI language, not the language selected in TMS settings. `chrome.runtime.getManifest()` turned out to apply the same browser-locale substitution to its `__MSG_xxx__` placeholders, so reading the description back from the manifest didn't help either. Descriptions are now re-resolved via a hardcoded command-name → message-key map (mirroring `manifest.json`'s `commands` section) passed through `gsUtils.getMessage()`, so they follow the same locale as the rest of the page. The browser-generated `_execute_action` description ("Activate the extension…") is not backed by a manifest message key and still follows the browser's language.
-- **Unhandled "Could not establish connection" promise rejections in the background service worker** (`tgs.js`): three `chrome.tabs.sendMessage()` calls (`updateCommand`, `initSettings`, `showNoConnectivityMessage`) had no `.catch()`, unlike the equivalent call for `initTab`. Since the target tab can be closed or navigated away between the `contextGetByTabId` check and the actual send, the resulting rejection surfaced as an uncaught error in the console. All three now catch and log the error like the existing pattern.
-- **Health page: Brave grouped-tab repair no longer silently fails** (`health.js`, `gsChrome.js`): the Brave recovery path now searches *all* saved sessions (not just the most recent one) for matching suspended URLs, fixing the case where `fetchLastSession()` returned `null` or the window-rank matching found no data. Added a visible warning when tabs cannot be recovered ("N tab(s) could not be recovered: no matching session data found."). Fixed `gsChrome.tabsGroup` to properly `return` after the `groupId === -1` guard and added `try/catch + reject` so failures on browsers that don't support `chrome.tabs.group` (Brave pre-v150) are caught and logged instead of silently hanging the repair flow; in that case the suspended tab is still created and the broken one removed, even if it can't be added back to the group.
-- **Local backups could rotate one file too early** (`gsBackup.js`): backup filenames used minute resolution (`...T14-30.json`) and `chrome.downloads.download` runs with `conflictAction: 'overwrite'`, so two backups fired in the same minute (e.g. a scheduled alarm plus a manual "Backup now") produced the same filename — one file on disk but two distinct download IDs tracked in `tmsLocalBackupIds`. This overcounted backups and made rotation delete a still-wanted file one cycle early. Timestamps now include seconds; the filename regexes accept the seconds segment as optional so existing minute-only files still match for migration/cleanup.
-- **Importing a malformed backup crashed with a raw TypeError** (`gsBackup.js`): `importBackupJson` only guarded `JSON.parse` and the top-level `windows` array, then iterated `win.tabs` unconditionally — a structurally valid but malformed file (a window with no `tabs` array) threw an uncaught `TypeError` instead of the clean `TMS_IMPORT_EMPTY`. Malformed windows are now skipped, and the import throws `TMS_IMPORT_EMPTY` if no usable window remains.
-- **`unsuspendWhitelistedTabs` could throw when no window is focused** (`tgs.js`): `chrome.windows.getLastFocused` can hand back `undefined`, and `tab.windowId === currentWindow.id` then threw inside the async `chrome.tabs.query` callback as an unhandled rejection. The focused window id is now read with optional chaining (`currentWindow?.id`).
